    "chain": "Okay, so I need to prove that (A ∪ B) - C is equal to (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Hmm, set operations can be a bit tricky, but I think I can handle this by using the definitions provided. Let me recall that for any set X and Y, x ∈ (X - Y) if and only if x is in X and x is not in Y. So, I need to show that every element in (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), and vice versa. That should establish the equality.\n\nFirst, let me write down what each side means using the definition. \n\nStarting with the left-hand side: (A ∪ B) - C. By definition, an element x is in (A ∪ B) - C if and only if x is in (A ∪ B) and x is not in C. \n\nNow, (A ∪ B) is the set of all elements that are in A or in B or in both. So, x ∈ (A ∪ B) means x is in A or x is in B. Therefore, (A ∪ B) - C is the set of all elements that are in A or B, but not in C.\n\nOn the other hand, the right-hand side is (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Let's break that down. (A - C) is the set of elements in A that are not in C, and (B - C) is the set of elements in B that are not in C. So, their union is all elements that are either in A but not in C, or in B but not in C, or in both.\n\nSo, to show that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C), I need to show two inclusions: \n\n1. (A ∪ B) - C is a subset of (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n2. (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is a subset of (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nLet me start with the first inclusion: (A ∪ B) - C ⊆ (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nTake any element x in (A ∪ B) - C. By definition, x is in A ∪ B and x is not in C. Since x is in A ∪ B, it must be in A or in B. So, there are two cases:\n\nCase 1: x is in A. Since x is not in C, x is in A - C. Therefore, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nCase 2: x is in B. Similarly, since x is not in C, x is in B - C. Therefore, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nIn both cases, x is in the union (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Therefore, every element of (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), which proves the first inclusion.\n\nNow, for the second inclusion: (A - C) ∪ (B - C) ⊆ (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nTake any element x in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). This means that x is either in A - C or in B - C. So, there are two cases again:\n\nCase 1: x is in A - C. Then, x is in A and x is not in C. Since x is in A, it is certainly in A ∪ B, and since x is not in C, x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nCase 2: x is in B - C. Similarly, x is in B and x is not in C. Therefore, x is in B ∪ A (since union is commutative), and x is not in C, so x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nIn both cases, x is in (A ∪ B) - C. Therefore, every element of (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is also in (A ∪ B) - C, which proves the second inclusion.\n\nSince both inclusions have been established, we can conclude that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C). \n\nWait, let me double-check if I missed anything. I considered all possible cases for x being in A or B, and in each case, I showed that x must be in one of the sets on the right-hand side. Also, for the second inclusion, I considered x being in A or B, and in each case, it's in the union on the left-hand side. Yeah, that seems solid. I don't think I made any logical errors here.\n\nJust to recap:\n\n1. For (A ∪ B) - C ⊆ (A - C) ∪ (B - C):\n   - If x ∈ (A ∪ B) - C, then x ∈ A ∪ B and x ∉ C.\n   - So, x ∈ A or x ∈ B, and x ∉ C.\n   - Therefore, x ∈ A - C or x ∈ B - C, which means x ∈ (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\n2. For (A - C) ∪ (B - C) ⊆ (A ∪ B) - C:\n   - If x ∈ (A - C) ∪ (B - C), then x ∈ A - C or x ∈ B - C.\n   - So, x ∈ A and x ∉ C, or x ∈ B and x ∉ C.\n   - Therefore, x ∈ A ∪ B and x ∉ C, which means x ∈ (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nYep, that all checks out. I think I've got it right. So, the equality holds because each side can be broken down into the same cases, and in each case, the elements are accounted for correctly in both sets.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equality is proven, so \\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}.\n</think>\n\nTo prove that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), we need to show mutual subset inclusion.\n\nFirst, we show that \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\):\n\n1. Take any element \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\). By definition, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\).\n2. Since \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\), \\(x\\) is in \\(A\\) or \\(B\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in A\\), then \\(x \\in A - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n4. If \\(x \\in B\\), then \\(x \\in B - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A \\cup B) - C\\) is in \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), proving \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nNext, we show that \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\):\n\n1. Take any element \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\). This means \\(x \\in A - C\\) or \\(x \\in B - C\\).\n2. If \\(x \\in A - C\\), then \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in B - C\\), then \\(x \\in B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\) is in \\((A \\cup B) - C\\), proving \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nSince both inclusions have been established, we conclude that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}\n\\]",
